WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.900
The President of the United States is in some
hot water… with HBO.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:09.180
In conjunction with Attorney General Bill
Barr's press conference discussing the release

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:13.969
of the redacted Mueller report, Donald Trump
took to Twitter with a Game of Thrones-inspired

00:00:13.969 --> 00:00:16.800
image that quickly drew the ire of the cable
channel.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.440
Its overlaid text, in a very Thrones-like
font, reads:

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:21.700
"No collusion.

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:22.740
No obstruction.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:26.500
For the haters and the radical left Democrats,
Game Over."

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:30.609
Game of Thrones fever is at an all-time high
at the moment, with the first episode of the

00:00:30.609 --> 00:00:33.380
venerable series' final season having just
aired.

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:37.980
As Deadline reports, HBO slyly referenced
this fact in a statement following the president's

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:38.840
tweet:

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:52.200
This is hardly the first time Trump's gotten
blowback for co-opting intellectual property

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:53.680
to make a political statement.

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:57.639
In fact, it's not even the first time he's
used Game of Thrones to do so.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.580
Last November, he tweeted an image of himself
with the text,

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:02.140
"Sanctions are coming."

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:06.000
Again, the slogan was rendered in a terribly
familiar font.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.780
And again, it prompted an epic response from
HBO's official Twitter account:

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:18.200
That unmistakably snarky comeback didn't keep
Trump from basically pulling the same shenanigans

00:01:18.219 --> 00:01:19.729
in January of this year.

00:01:19.729 --> 00:01:24.200
He posted another image that owed its aesthetic
to Game of Thrones, along with the caption:

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:26.020
"The wall is coming."

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:30.960
Following the November incident, HBO released
a statement saying they weren't aware of the

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:32.900
messaging but didn't approve of it.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:37.320
Nevertheless, the network's spokesperson Jeff
Cusson implied that no legal action would

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:38.320
be taken.

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:42.500
At the end of the day, the case could be made
that Trump's tweets are parody, which is protected

00:01:42.509 --> 00:01:43.939
under the Fair Use act.

00:01:43.939 --> 00:01:48.149
Even, or perhaps especially, if the tweets
are coming from someone who occupies the highest

00:01:48.149 --> 00:01:49.700
office in all the land.

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:54.180
Apparently, the president's legal team is
only vaguely aware of the subtleties involved

00:01:54.189 --> 00:01:55.460
in copyright law.

00:01:55.460 --> 00:02:00.680
After all, it's been barely a week since another
Trump tweet misappropriated intellectual property…

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:03.020
and that tweet wound up being removed.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:08.200
On April 9, the president posted a video hyping
his 2020 re-election campaign.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:13.430
It just so happened to be scored to "Why Do
We Fall?," a classical piece by Hans Zimmer

00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:17.440
composed for the 2012 superhero flick The
Dark Knight Rises.

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:22.420
The video incorporated text rendered in a
font that was suspiciously similar to those

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:23.760
in the film's credits.

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:28.000
Warner Brothers, the studio behind the Dark
Knight trilogy, quickly responded with a curt

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:29.220
statement of their own:

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:41.840
Parody is one thing, but as any rapper will
tell you, you can't just jack other peoples'

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:44.960
tunes for your own purposes... unless you
want to end up in court.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:49.340
While HBO may not have a case for trademark
infringement, some legal analysts believe

00:02:49.349 --> 00:02:54.180
they could argue that the president's usage
of Game of Thrones imagery constituted trademark

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:56.660
dilution; an entirely separate concept.

00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:01.400
Anyway, it's hard to imagine any Game of Thrones
fan thinking the image was proof-positive

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:05.940
of HBO and the show's cast and crew endorsing
the president's opinion of the Mueller report.

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:10.360
We're not in the business of taking political
stances here at Looper, but we are in the

00:03:10.379 --> 00:03:13.019
business of taking a stance on Game of Thrones.

00:03:13.019 --> 00:03:16.739
With that in mind, we'd like to point out
that the president's tweet doesn't make much

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:18.800
sense in the context of the series.

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:22.980
Unless we missed a crucial episode, we're
pretty sure there aren't any Democrats in

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:23.980
Westeros.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:28.180
We suppose there are plenty of "haters" on
the show, though perhaps not in the way Trump

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:28.980
meant.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:40.239
Regardless, there's no argument that the series
will be coming to a close at the end of May.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:42.500
So indeed, the Game is nearly over.

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:45.720
Well, like the old adage says, two out of
three ain't bad.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:46.980
Or is it?

